I certainly don't remember writing it so I doubt it was me, but simply
changing;

        boolean skip_objects = (obj_cnt >= 5), felt_cockatrice = FALSE;

to

        boolean skip_objects = (obj_cnt >= 2), felt_cockatrice = FALSE;

in invent.c will do this. Note that this suppresses trap notifications
(other than those from actual trap effects) whenever there are 2 or more
objects; removing the;

!skip_objects &&

in

        if (!skip_objects && (trap = t_at(u.ux,u.uy)) && trap->tseen)

later looks harmless to me.

This will cause 2 objects to be described as "several". If you want to get
really clever you could mess around with;

            There("are %s%s objects here.",
                  (obj_cnt <= 10) ? "several" : "many",

like so;

            There("are %s%s objects here.",
                  (obj_cnt<=5) ? "some" : (obj_cnt <= 10) ? "several" : "many",

not that any of this is tested. This does have a game balance effect in
that a character who walks over a cockatrice corpse while blind without
gloves will not die when there is 1 other object there rather than 4.
